---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';

const entries = await getCollection('books');
const books = entries
  .map((e) => e.data)
  .sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Copyright and attribution"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles copyright: we host no copyrighted material and link only to rights holders' own servers.`}
>
  <div class="wrap book-header">
    <h1>Copyright and attribution</h1>
    <p class="book-header__lede">
      This site exists to make legally free study material easier to follow. It
      works only if it stays scrupulous about what it does and does not host.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose" style="margin-top: var(--space-5)">
    <h2>What we host</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No copyrighted books, chapters, or PDFs.</strong> Not mirrored,
      not cached, not proxied, not embedded. This site is a set of ordered
      links and our own written commentary. If you inspect the repository you
      will find no book files in it, and none are served from our domain.
    </p>

    <h2>What we link to</h2>
    <p>
      Every reading link points at the server of the author, the publisher, or
      the university that chose to publish that material freely — typically{' '}
      <code>math.mit.edu</code> or <code>ocw.mit.edu</code>. Following a link
      takes you to them, not to a copy of ours. Linking to a publicly published
      page requires no licence, but we label every link with its source so you
      always know whose material you are opening.
    </p>
    <p>
      We link only to material the rights holder published for free access. We
      do not link to unauthorised copies, file lockers, or shadow libraries,
      and we will not accept contributions that do.
    </p>

    <h2>Book attribution</h2>
    <p>Each book covered here, and the free course it maps onto:</p>
    <ul>
      {
        books.map((book) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{book.title}</strong> © {book.year} {book.author}
            {book.publisher ? `, ${book.publisher}` : ''}. Official page:{' '}
            <a href={book.officialPage} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              {book.officialPage.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')}
            </a>
            .
            {book.course && (
              <>
                {' '}Lecture and problem links go to {book.course.name}
                {book.course.license && (
                  <>
                    , used under{' '}
                    {book.course.licenseUrl ? (
                      <a
                        href={book.course.licenseUrl}
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener"
                      >
                        {book.course.license}
                      </a>
                    ) : (
                      book.course.license
                    )}
                  </>
                )}
                .
              </>
            )}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Course material from MIT OpenCourseWare is used under its Creative
      Commons licence, which permits non-commercial reuse with attribution.
      This site is non-commercial: it carries no advertising, sells nothing,
      and accepts no payment.
    </p>

    <h2>Book cover art</h2>
    <p>
      We do not reproduce publishers' cover designs. The covers you see on this
      site are plain typographic placeholders we generate ourselves from the
      book's title.
    </p>

    <h2>Our own writing</h2>
    <p>
      The study guides, section commentary, and roadmap structure are original
      work written for this site. They are released under{' '}
      <a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a
      >, so you may reuse them with attribution. They summarise and comment on
      the books in our own words — they do not reproduce the text of any book.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site's source code is released under the MIT licence and is available
      at <a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repo.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Please buy the books</h2>
    <p>
      A roadmap through free lecture material is a supplement to a book, not a
      substitute for one. Where a book is sold, we link to buy it. If a book
      here is useful to you and you can afford a copy, buying it is what keeps
      authors writing them.
    </p>

    <h2>If you are a rights holder</h2>
    <p>
      If you believe anything on this site links to material that should not be
      publicly available, or you would like a link to your work removed for any
      reason, email{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. We will
      act on it promptly — a good-faith request is enough, and we will not ask
      you to justify it. You can also open an issue on{' '}
      <a href={SITE.repo}>the repository</a> if you prefer a public record.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
